@node LQL Watches
@chapter LQL Watches: Standing Queries

@cindex LQL watches
@cindex standing queries
@cindex subscriptions, LQL
@findex cmacs-lql-watch
@findex cmacs-lql-define-watch

A query run with @code{cmacs-lql-run} or @kbd{M-x cmacs-lql-query}
answers once.  A @dfn{watch} is a query you register once and that
calls you when its answer changes.  A podomation trigger, a bar item
and an agent's ``tell me when X'' are then all the same thing: a query,
and what to do about a change.

@lisp
(cmacs-lql-define-watch dead-processes
  "fetch processes | filter status == \"exit\" | fields name | sort name"
  :every 30 :key "name"
  :on-change (lambda (_new _old diff)
               (dolist (row (plist-get diff :added))
                 (message "%s exited" (cdr (assoc "name" row))))))
@end lisp

Four things about it, stated plainly:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@emph{A change is a change in the result, never the passing of time.}
The result is hashed; a watch fires only when the hash differs from the
last one.  The first result is the baseline and fires nothing unless
the watch says @code{:immediate}.
@item
@emph{It polls.}  LQL has no notion of a source announcing a change, so
a watch re-runs its query every @code{:every} seconds.  Everything in
@ref{LQL Watch Cost} exists to make that invisible.
@item
@emph{A watch never gets in your way.}  One watch query runs at a time,
none while you are running a query of your own, and none that touches
editor state while you are typing.
@item
@emph{An agent's watch sees what the agent may see, and no more.}
@xref{LQL Watch Agents}.
@end itemize

Files: @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-lql-watch.el} (the watches and the list
buffer), @file{cmacs-lql-watch-sinks.el} (podomation, the bar, toasts),
@file{cmacs-lql-watch-tools.el} (the agent tools).  There is no C: a
watch is built on @code{cmacs-lql-run}.  Requires
@option{--with-cmacs-lql}.

