@node LQL Watch Definition
@section Defining a Watch

@code{(cmacs-lql-watch @var{name} @var{query} &rest @var{options})}
registers a watch and returns its name as a string; @var{name} may be a
string or a symbol.  @code{(cmacs-lql-define-watch @var{name}
@var{query} @var{options}@dots{})} is the same for an init file:
@var{name} is unquoted, and in a cmacs built without LQL it prints a
message instead of failing the init file.  Registering a name again
replaces that watch.

@table @code
@item :every
Seconds between runs.  Default @code{cmacs-lql-watch-default-interval}
(60); never below @code{cmacs-lql-watch-min-interval} (5), a smaller
value is raised.
@item :on-change
Function of (@var{new-rows} @var{old-rows} @var{diff}), called when the
result changes.
@item :key
A column name, or a list of them, that identifies a row; lets the diff
tell a changed row from one removed and one added.
@item :on-rows
Function of (@var{new-rows}), called on a change that leaves the result
non-empty: ``when there is something''.
@item :immediate
Fire on the first result too, with @var{old-rows} @code{nil}.
@item :depends
When a tick may be skipped without running: @code{auto} (the default),
@code{sources} or @code{always}.  @xref{LQL Watch Cost}.
@item :max-rows
Rows kept and compared; default and ceiling
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-max-rows} (500), itself never above
@code{cmacs-lql-max-rows}.
@item :timeout
Seconds one run may take; default @code{cmacs-lql-watch-timeout} (30).
@item :paused
Register it paused.
@item :event
Emit a podomation event on a change.  @xref{LQL Watch Podomation}.
@item :bar
Drive a gowl bar item.  @xref{LQL Watch Bar}.
@item :toast
A bar toast on a change; @code{t} or a template.
@end table

An option that is none of these is an error at registration: a typo in
@code{:on-change} must not produce a watch that silently does nothing.

@subheading Rows and the diff

Rows reach a callback as alists of @code{(@var{column-name}
. @var{value})}, the column name a string: @code{(cdr (assoc "name"
row))}.  NULL is @code{nil} and false is @code{:false}, as
@code{cmacs-lql-parse} has them.

@var{diff} is a plist, also available as @code{(cmacs-lql-watch-diff
@var{old} @var{new} &optional @var{key})}:

@table @code
@item :added
Rows in the new result and not the old.
@item :removed
Rows in the old result and not the new.
@item :changed
With @code{:key} only: @code{(@var{old-row} . @var{new-row})} pairs
whose key stayed and whose other columns differ.
@item :reordered
@code{t} when the same rows came back in a different order and nothing
else differs.
@end table

Without @code{:key} rows are compared whole, as a multiset, so a row
that changed shows as one removed and one added.

Any difference in the result fires, including order.  @emph{Write a
query whose result is small and stable}: filter, summarize, and sort.
@samp{fetch processes} changes every time a process's status string
does; @samp{fetch processes | filter status == "exit" | fields name |
sort name} changes when something exits.

@subheading Other functions

@table @code
@item cmacs-lql-unwatch @var{name}
Remove a watch; a run in flight is cancelled and its result ignored.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-list &optional @var{owner}
Every watch as a plist: @code{:name :query :every :owner :state :rows
:total :runs :changes :failures :skipped :last-skip :last-run
:last-change :next-due :last-error :callback-error :callback-seconds}.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-get @var{name} @var{property}
One of those, or any option the watch was registered with.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-rows @var{name}
The rows of the last result.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-run-now @var{name}
Make it due now, clearing a backoff.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-pause @var{name}
@itemx cmacs-lql-watch-resume @var{name}
Stop and restart it.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-diff-json @var{diff} &optional @var{max}
The diff as bounded JSON, for anything that carries text.
@end table

Hooks, for code that wants every watch rather than one:
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-change-functions} (@var{name} @var{new} @var{old}
@var{diff}), @code{cmacs-lql-watch-baseline-functions} (@var{name}
@var{rows}; the first result, which is not a change),
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-error-functions} (@var{name} @var{message}; once
when a watch starts failing) and
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-removed-functions} (@var{name} @var{owner}).  A
consumer that gives watches a new option adds its keyword to
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-extra-keys}.  The three shipped consumers are
written with exactly these and nothing private.

@node LQL Watch List
@section The List: @kbd{M-x cmacs-lql-watches}

@findex cmacs-lql-watches

One line per watch: name, state, interval, rows, runs, changes, when it
last ran, when it last changed, owner, and the query---or, for a watch
that is failing, its error in place of the query.

@table @kbd
@item r
Run it now (at the next good moment; @pxref{LQL Watch Cost}).
@item p
Pause, or resume.
@item d
@itemx k
Remove it.
@item s
Save it to @code{cmacs-lql-watch-saved}.
@item a
Add one: name, query, interval; it toasts on a change.
@item @key{RET}
Everything about it: query, counters, errors, last rows.
@item g
Redraw.
@end table

States: @code{waiting} (no result yet), @code{ok}, @code{running},
@code{paused}, @code{error} (@samp{error x3} after three failures in a
row).  The buffer redraws itself after each run while it is visible.

@node LQL Watch Cost
@section What It Costs

@cindex LQL watches, cost

LQL runs one query at a time on one worker thread, and an Elisp-defined
source (@code{buffers}, @code{notes}, @code{activity}, @dots{}) is
snapshotted to JSON on the editor thread before the query is submitted.
A watch is therefore work you did not ask for at that moment, twice
over.  The rules:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@emph{One timer} for every watch, armed for the earliest one due.  No
watches, no timer.
@item
@emph{One watch query in flight}, across all watches.  Queued watch
queries would sit in front of the query you are about to run; one
cannot delay you by more than its own run.  When it lands the next due
watch goes.
@item
@emph{Never queued twice.}  A watch whose last run has not come back is
not submitted again.  A run that outlives its timeout by ten seconds is
cancelled and counted as a failure, so one stuck query cannot stop the
rest.
@item
@emph{You come first.}  Before submitting, the watch asks the engine's
try-lock helper whether a query is running (the @code{cmacs-lql-busy}
signal).  If one is, the tick is skipped and retried in
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-busy-retry} seconds (2).
@item
@emph{Not under your fingers.}  A watch that names an Elisp source
waits until Emacs has been idle for @code{cmacs-lql-watch-min-idle}
seconds (0.75).  Watches that read only engine sources---files, git,
the journal, SQL---are not held back, since nothing of theirs runs on
the editor thread.
@item
@emph{Errors back off.}  A failing watch waits twice as long after each
failure in a row, starting from its own interval, up to
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-max-backoff} (an hour).  You get one message when
it starts failing; after that the error is in the list.  A success
resets it.
@item
@emph{Caps.}  @code{cmacs-lql-watch-max} watches (32),
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-max-per-owner} per agent task (4),
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-max-rows} rows, and
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-max-result-bytes} of result JSON (2 MiB; a bigger
result is an error on the watch, not a diff).
@item
@emph{An unchanged result costs a hash.}  The result is parsed into
rows only when its hash differs.
@end itemize

@subheading Skipping on source generations

A source with a generation function
(@code{cmacs-lql-set-source-generation}: @code{notes} and its siblings,
@code{activity}) can say cheaply whether its rows would differ.  When
@emph{every} Elisp source a watch names has one and none has moved
since the last successful run, the tick is skipped outright: no
snapshot, no submit.

That is only sound if the query reads nothing else, so with
@code{:depends auto} the skip also requires that the query mention no
engine source or source command (checked against the engine's own list,
not assumed) and nothing matching
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-volatile-regexp} (@code{now}, @code{today},
@code{random}, @dots{}).  And because it is still an inference, it is
bounded: after @code{cmacs-lql-watch-max-generation-skips} skips in a
row (20) the watch runs anyway.  A wrong inference can make a change
late; it cannot lose one.

@code{:depends sources} trusts the generations regardless of what else
the query reads.  @code{:depends always} never skips; use it for a
query whose result depends on the clock in a way the regexp does not
catch.

@node LQL Watch Callbacks
@section Callbacks

Callbacks run on the main thread, when the result arrives.  Three
guarantees and one limit:

@itemize @bullet
@item
An error in a callback is caught and recorded on the watch
(@code{:callback-error}, shown in the list).  It is never signalled
into the timer or into the GLib dispatch that delivered the result, and
it does not stop the other consumers of the same change.
@item
Callbacks run with @code{inhibit-interaction} bound to @code{t}.  A
watch fires at a moment nobody chose, and a callback that opened the
minibuffer would take the keyboard from whatever you were doing---under
@command{emacs --gowl}, from the desktop.  A callback that prompts gets
an @code{inhibited-interaction} error, recorded like any other.
@item
A side effect of that binding: inside a callback you look like a
program to anything that asks (the activity journal does).  A callback
that itself queries @code{activity} gets no rows unless
@code{cmacs-journal-expose-to-agents}.  Use the rows you were handed.
@item
The limit: nothing can preempt a callback that computes for a long
time.  Keep callbacks quick; long work is a process with a sentinel.
@code{:callback-seconds} records how long the last one took.
@end itemize

@node LQL Watch Podomation
@section podomation

@cindex podomation, LQL watch events

A watch with @code{:event t} emits the event @code{on_lql_watch}
through the built-in @code{cmacs} module on every change, when the
podomation engine is running.  Every field is a string.

@table @code
@item watch
The watch's name.
@item rows
Rows in the new result.
@item total
The result's own total (rows before the row cap).
@item added
@itemx removed
@itemx changed
How many rows of each.
@item row
The first row as a JSON object, or empty.
@item diff
@code{@{"added": [...], "removed": [...], "changed": [@{"old": ...,
"new": ...@}], "counts": @{...@}@}}, cut to
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-event-diff-max} characters (4096) with
@code{"truncated": true} when cut; @code{counts} always has the real
sizes.
@end table

A worked example.  In @file{init.el}:

@lisp
(cmacs-lql-define-watch inbox
  "fetch notes | filter para == \"inbox\" | summarize n = count()"
  :every 300 :event t)

(cmacs-lql-define-watch dead-processes
  "fetch processes | filter status == \"exit\" | fields name | sort name"
  :every 30 :key "name" :event t)
@end lisp

In a @file{.pod} file:

@example
pod c = cmacs->new();

/* The inbox count moved: say so. */
c->on_lql_watch where event->watch == "inbox"
    => cmacs->notify("Notes inbox", "now @{event->row@}");

/* A process died: keep a log, with the rows that appeared. */
c->on_lql_watch where event->watch == "dead-processes" && event->added != "0"
    => log->write_warning("processes exited: @{event->diff@}");
@end example

There is one event name for every watch, told apart by
@code{event->watch}: a module's events are a fixed list and a watch's
name is not.  Binding works whether or not the @code{cmacs} module's
list names the event; the list is what the podomation REPL completes
and prints.

@node LQL Watch Bar
@section The gowl Bar

@cindex gowl bar, LQL watch items
@findex cmacs-lql-watch-bar-place

A watch with @code{:bar} sets the @code{text} setting of a bar widget
on its first result and on every change, through
@code{cmacs-gowl-bar-push}---the same push every Emacs-owned bar item
uses.  With no bar running it does nothing.

@lisp
(cmacs-lql-watch-bar-place "label")   ; once: add the widget, right region
(cmacs-lql-define-watch inbox
  "fetch notes | filter para == \"inbox\" | summarize n = count()"
  :every 300
  :bar '(:widget "label" :format "inbox @{n@}" :empty ""))
@end lisp

@code{:bar} is a widget name or a plist: @code{:widget},
@code{:format} (a template, or a function of the rows returning the
text; default @code{"@{name@}: @{rows@}"}) and @code{:empty} (the text
for a result with no rows).  In a template @code{@{rows@}} is the row
count, @code{@{total@}} the result's total, @code{@{name@}} the watch's
name and @code{@{@var{column}@}} that column of the first row.  The
text is one line, cut at @code{cmacs-lql-watch-bar-max-chars} (80).

@code{cmacs-lql-watch-bar-place @var{widget} &optional @var{region}}
adds a widget with the bar's amendment keys (@code{widgets-right-add}
and friends), so the rest of your layout is left alone.  @var{region}
is @code{left}, @code{center}, @code{right} (the default) or those
prefixed @code{bottom-}.

@emph{A limit you should know about.}  The only shipped widget whose
text can be set from outside is @code{label} (alias @code{text}), and
its text comes from its spec parameter before its @code{text}
setting---so @code{label:something} cannot be driven, only a bare
@code{label} and a bare @code{text}.  That is @emph{two} watch-driven
bar items without writing a plugin.  Lifting it is a two-line change in
gowl's @code{label_configure} (prefer the @code{text} setting over the
spec parameter, as the comment in @file{cmacs-lql-watch-sinks.el}
describes); a bar plugin of your own that reads a @code{text} setting
has no such limit.

@code{:toast t} raises a bar toast on every change (@samp{@var{name}
changed} and the counts), or a message where there is no bar.
@code{:toast "template"} uses a template for the summary.

@node LQL Watch Agents
@section Agents

@cindex brigade tools, LQL watches
@cindex lql_watch

Three brigade tools, in @file{cmacs-lql-watch-tools.el}:

@table @code
@item lql_watch
Parameters @code{name}, @code{query}, @code{every} (optional),
@code{key} (optional).  Register a watch; the agent is told when its
result changes.
@item lql_unwatch
Parameter @code{name}.  Remove one of its own.
@item lql_watches
No parameters.  Its own watches: state, counts, last error, undelivered
news.
@end table

The rules, all enforced on the editor's side:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@emph{The owner is the task, and the task is not something the model
says.}  It is the brigade task the tool call arrived on behalf of,
noted from @code{cmacs-brigade-before-tool-call-functions} exactly as
the desktop tools do.  A call from no task (a chat buffer, an external
MCP client, @command{emacsctl}) is refused: there would be nobody to
tell and no run whose end removes the watch.
@item
@emph{A watch's real name is @samp{@var{task}/@var{name}}.}  An agent
can neither replace nor remove a watch that is not its own, whatever it
calls it; and core refuses to let any owner replace another owner's
watch by name.
@item
@emph{Bounded.}  At most @code{cmacs-lql-watch-max-per-owner} watches
per task, never faster than @code{cmacs-lql-watch-tools-min-interval}
seconds (30; default 120).
@item
@emph{Removed when the run ends}
(@code{cmacs-brigade-run-finished-functions}).
@item
@emph{No callbacks, no podomation event, no bar item.}  The tools pass
none of those options, and the sinks ignore any watch that has an
owner.
@item
@emph{Delivery} is a message to the task's mailbox
(@code{cmacs-brigade-mailbox-send}), which steers a running agent and
wakes a parked one.  At most one undelivered message per watch is
queued: a watch that changes ten times while its agent is busy costs it
one message, and @code{lql_watches} has the rest.  A watch that starts
failing tells its agent once.
@end itemize

@subheading The activity journal

A one-shot @code{lql_query} from an agent gets no @code{activity} rows
unless @code{cmacs-journal-expose-to-agents}, because the source's
snapshot is taken inside the tool call and the journal sees that.  A
watch runs later, from a timer, where nothing marks the caller as an
agent---so a naive watch would have been a way around the setting.  It
is not, twice over:

@enumerate
@item
An owned watch is submitted---and its sources snapshotted, and their
generation functions asked---inside the same two bindings a tool call
runs in (@code{cmacs-journal--in-tool-call} and
@code{inhibit-interaction}).  Every source that decides ``is an agent
asking?'' gets the same answer for the watch as for the agent's own
query.  This covers any source, present or future, not only
@code{activity}.
@item
Registering an owned watch whose query names a source listed in
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-agent-gated-sources} (@code{activity} ->
@code{cmacs-journal-expose-to-agents}) is refused outright, with the
reason, while that variable is nil.  This is the lock that explains
itself: the agent is told why instead of being handed a watch that is
always empty.
@end enumerate

Your own watches are submitted as you, and see the journal.

@node LQL Watch Saving
@section Saving Watches

@findex cmacs-lql-watch-save
@findex cmacs-lql-watch-restore-saved

Watches defined in your init file are code.  One made interactively
(@kbd{a} in the list, or @code{cmacs-lql-watch-add}) can be saved with
@kbd{s} (@code{cmacs-lql-watch-save}) into the option
@code{cmacs-lql-watch-saved}, a list of @code{(@var{name} @var{query}
. @var{options})}.  Only a watch whose options are plain data can be
saved---a named function is fine, a lambda is not.  Saved watches are
registered by @code{cmacs-lql-watch-restore-saved}, which you call from
your init file:

@lisp
(when IS-CMACS-LQL (cmacs-lql-watch-restore-saved))
@end lisp

An entry that cannot be registered is reported and skipped; the others
still are.

@node LQL Watch Options
@section Options

@table @code
@item cmacs-lql-watch-default-interval
Interval of a watch that names none.  Default 60.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-min-interval
Floor on any interval.  Default 5.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-min-idle
Idle seconds before snapshotting an Elisp source; 0 turns the wait off.
Default 0.75.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-max
Watches in total.  Default 32.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-max-per-owner
Watches per agent task.  Default 4.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-max-rows
Rows kept and compared.  Default 500.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-max-result-bytes
Largest result kept.  Default 2 MiB.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-timeout
Seconds per run.  Default 30.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-max-backoff
Longest wait between runs of a failing watch.  Default 3600.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-busy-retry
Wait after finding the engine busy.  Default 2.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-max-generation-skips
Skips in a row before running anyway; 0 never skips.  Default 20.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-volatile-regexp
Query text that rules out skipping (@code{now}, @code{today}, @dots{}).
@item cmacs-lql-watch-agent-gated-sources
Sources an agent's watch may name only when a variable allows
(@code{activity}).
@item cmacs-lql-watch-event-name
The podomation event.  Default @code{on_lql_watch}.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-event-diff-max
Characters of diff in an event.  Default 4096.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-bar-max-chars
Longest bar text.  Default 80.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-tools-min-interval
Floor on an agent's interval.  Default 30.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-tools-default-interval
An agent's interval when it names none.  Default 120.
@item cmacs-lql-watch-tools-max-chars
Characters of diff in a message to an agent.  Default 4000.
@end table

@subheading Tests

@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-lql-watch-tests.el} needs no engine:
@code{cmacs-lql-run} is replaced by a stub that hands the test the
callback, the timer is never armed, and the clock is the test's.  It
covers the baseline, a keyed diff, the in-flight rule, the watchdog,
the busy skip, the typing skip, the generation skip and its bound,
backoff and its cap, contained callbacks (a prompt included), the caps,
ownership, the journal gate from both directions, the agent tools'
delivery and coalescing, and each sink.
